\title{NSCA: Necessary and Sufficient Condition Analysis}
\description{
Tests whether a condition is both necessary and sufficient for an outcome, that is, whether the outcome cannot be reached without the condition and is guaranteed in its presence. Such a claim implies that two diagonally opposite corners of the condition-outcome scatter plot contain no observations. The package estimates both boundaries with the same data, scope and frontier technique, reports the effect size and permutation test of each, and indicates joint support only when both components are supported. The two components are summarized by the weaker effect, a balanced combination and the proportion of the scope that the two claims jointly rule out. A dual-threshold table gives, for each outcome level, the condition level it requires, the level sufficient for it, and the interval between them. Results can be compared with regression slopes. The package also provides plots, influential-case diagnostics, scope-sensitivity and resampling checks, and simulation and power analysis for planning studies. The two sides are estimated with the 'NCA' and 'SCAtools' packages.
}
\section{The method}{

A condition is necessary for an outcome when the outcome does not occur
without it, and sufficient when the condition guarantees the outcome. In a
scatter plot of condition against outcome, each claim rules out one corner.
Necessary Condition Analysis (NCA; Dul, 2016) estimates the empty corner of
a necessity claim, and Sufficient Condition Analysis, implemented in the
SCAtools package, estimates the empty corner of a sufficiency claim. A
necessary-and-sufficient claim rules out both corners, which lie diagonally
opposite each other, so NSCA estimates both on the same observations, scope
and frontier technique and assesses them together. The band between the two
frontiers is the admissible region: the narrower it is, the closer the
relationship comes to a strict necessary-and-sufficient one. The framework,
and the vocabulary the tables use, follow Chan (in press), \emph{From Necessity to
Sufficiency and Necessity-and-Sufficiency: Extending Condition Analysis in
Degree}.
}

\section{What it is for}{

\itemize{
\item Testing the claim that a condition is both required and sufficient for an
outcome; the joint claim is supported only when both components are.
\item Estimating the thresholds: for each outcome level, the condition level
the outcome requires, the level that is sufficient for it, and the
interval between them (\code{\link[=nsca_thresholds]{nsca_thresholds()}}).
\item Describing how closely the relationship approaches a
necessary-and-sufficient one, through both component effect sizes and
three summaries that combine them (\code{\link[=nsca_table]{nsca_table()}}).
\item Comparing the result with regression (\code{\link[=nsca_compare]{nsca_compare()}}) and planning the
sample size of a study (\code{\link[=nsca_power]{nsca_power()}}).
}
}

